Verdict: **PLAUSIBLE**

Mechanism is real and confirmed in code, but I could not pin down a message sequence reachable through ordinary, single-session live usage of this app — it requires bringing in messages from outside the app's own continue()-call invariant (e.g. an imported/cross-version/CLI-originated session, or hand-edited history).

Key evidence:

`web/src/hooks/session-recovery.ts:47-54`:
```ts
export function prepareMessagesForRetry(messages: AgentMessage[]): AgentMessage[] | null {
	if (!isRetryableAssistantMessage(messages.at(-1))) return null;
	const remaining = messages.slice(0, -1).map(cloneMessage);
	const last = remaining.at(-1) as { role?: unknown } | undefined;
	return last?.role === "user" || last?.role === "user-with-attachments" || last?.role === "toolResult"
		? remaining
		: null;
}
```

`web/src/hooks/useKeatingAgent.tsx:798-806` (and the mirrored `retryExistingResponse` at 1332-1340):
```ts
const retryLastResponse = async () => {
  if (agent.state.isStreaming) return;
  const retryMessages = prepareMessagesForRetry(agent.state.messages);
  if (!retryMessages) return;
  await ensureSessionStartContext();
  agent.state.messages = retryMessages;
  await persistSnapshot();
  await agent.continue();
};
```
If `prepareMessagesForRetry` returns `null`, `retryLastResponse` returns silently — no error, no `agent.continue()`. `onAuthError` (line 814-827) still calls this and returns `true` regardless, so the auth dialog closes as if recovery succeeded, but the agent never resumes.

Confirmed via the patch (`/tmp/.../diff-tracked.patch` lines 3036-3051): the old code was
```ts
const messages = agent.state.messages;
const last = messages[messages.length - 1] as any;
if (last && last.role === "assistant" && last.stopReason === "error") {
  agent.state.messages = messages.slice(0, -1);
}
agent.continue().catch(...)
```
— it unconditionally called `agent.continue()` after an optional trim, with no precondition on the preceding message's role and no possibility of a silent no-op. The new code adds that precondition and the early-return-without-error path is new behavior.

Why not CONFIRMED: I traced every `agent.continue()` / `agent.send()` call site that the web app itself controls:
- Initial user send → preceded by `user`/`user-with-attachments`.
- Internal tool-calling loop (pi-agent-core) → preceded by `toolResult`.
- Capability-activation continuation (`useKeatingAgent.tsx:775-782`, `capabilityContinuationPending`) → only triggered after a capability-activating tool call, so also preceded by `toolResult`.
- Retry itself only proceeds when the precondition already holds.

So for a single live in-app session with no external session content, the invariant "assistant turn is always preceded by user/user-with-attachments/toolResult" appears to hold structurally, meaning `prepareMessagesForRetry` would not return `null` in practice for that case.

Why not REFUTED: `SessionData.messages` (loaded via `sessions.loadSession`, cross-device sync, or `web/src/keating/portable-data.ts` import) is stored/restored as raw `AgentMessage[]` with no shape validation or normalization on load (`portable-data.ts:91` just filters out `null` entries, nothing about message roles). The underlying `AgentMessage` type also includes CLI/harness-oriented roles not covered by the allow-list — `bashExecution`, `custom`, `branchSummary`, `compactionSummary` (`.../pi-agent-core/dist/harness/messages.d.ts`). If a session that ends in an errored/aborted assistant turn preceded by one of these (or by another `assistant` message, from a hand-edited/cross-version/CLI-originated session) is loaded into the web agent and the user then hits a subsequent auth error, `retryLastResponse` would silently no-op exactly as the candidate finding describes. I did not find or rule out a concrete UI action in this codebase that produces such a sequence purely through normal web-app use, so this remains unverified rather than refuted.
